      "textEn": "56\nThey were addressed as 'dvija', meaning 'twice-born'. Having gathered in Naimisharanya, the sages were listening to Srimad Bhagavatam from Sut Goswami.\nSince all the sages were Brahmins, but to acquire the qualification of a Brahmin,\nit is not enough to just take it. Just being a twice-born (Dvija) is not a qualification. Siddhi (spiritual power) is only attained when\none listens to the divine plays of God from an authentic source.\nOthers are merely praiseworthy, not truly virtuous.\nBy listening, one becomes delighted; what is the use of praising Srivatsa? 34). Meaning of the word\n\nOthers-- of others; Punyashlokanam-- of sacred fame; Udham-yashasam-- whose fame is spread everywhere;\nSatam- of the devotees; Upashrutyah-- having heard; Bhavet- can rise; Modah- joy; Srivatsa- of the one with the mark of the creeper; Adakshyas- of the one who wears; Kim- again-- what to say.\nIf a human wishes, they can rejoice by listening to the activities of the immortal, glorious devotees.\nThen, what is there to say about listening to the pastimes of Shri Vatsadhari Shri Bhagwan! Meaning: The literal meaning of Bhagavatam is the divine play of God and his devotees.\nFor example, the divine plays of Lord Krishna and the narratives of devotees like Ye, Dhruva, and Maharaj Ambarisha.\nThese two types of divine plays are related to the complete Supreme Being, because the divine plays of devotees\nare related to Him. For example, in the Mahabharata, the history of the Pandavas and their\nactivities are, therefore, sacred, because the Pandavas had a direct connection with Shri Bhagavan.\nYo Gajendram FUE Dhyayantam Charanambujam |\nKroshanim Karenunam Krushato Amayadadrutam | 35). Meaning of the word:\n\nYah- who; Gaj- elephant king; Ish- crocodile; Grasitam- attacked;\nDhyayantam- while meditating; Charan- feet; Ambujam- lotus; Kroshanitinam- lamenting; Karenunam-\nto the elephants; Krichharrutah- from danger; Amochayat- rescued; Dutam- quickly.\n\nThat Gajraj (elephant king) on whom the crocodile had attacked, and who then meditated on the lotus feet of the Lord, was immediately rescued by the Lord. At that time, his\nassistant maids were grumbling, but God saved them from the impending crisis"
