      "textEn": "6l\nKshatta--Vidura; Maha-bhagavat--The supreme devotee of the Lord; Krishnasya--belonging to Krishna; Ekantikih--one who is devoted;\nSuhit--friend; Yah--who; Tatyaj--renounced; Agra-jam--to his elder brother (King Dhritarashtra); Krishnae--to Krishna;\nSa-apatyam--to his hundred sons; Agha-van--the offender; Iti--Thus..\n\nShaunak Rishi wanted to know about Vidur, who was a great devotee of Lord Krishna\nand a friend, and who left his elder brother for the sake of God\nwho, along with his sons, conspired against the will of God.\n\nMeaning: Here is a mention of the event when Vidur, abandoning the shelter of his elder brother Dhritarashtra,\ntraveled to pilgrimage sites and finally met Maitreya in Haridwar. Here, Shaunak\nis inquiring about the topics of the conversation between Rishi, Vidur, and Maitreya. Vidur.\nHis specialty was that he was not only a friend of God, but also a great devotee.\nShrikrishna was trying to stop the war and to remove the discord between the cousins.\nBut when he did not heed their advice, Kshatta, that is, Vidur, became very unhappy and\nHe left that place. As a devotee, Vidur presented such an example that\nwherever there is no respect for Shri Krishna, that place is not suitable for humans to live. Even if a devotee\nis being tolerant about his personal interests, but if God or his devotees are being disrespected,\nhe should not remain tolerant. Here, the word 'Aghavan' is extremely important,\nbecause it indicates that the defeat of the Kauravas, the sons of Dhritarashtra, happened because\nthey violated the teachings of Shri Krishna and committed sinful deeds.\nDvāpāyanaadvaro mahitvē tasya dehajah |\nSarvatmānā śrītah krṣṇam tatparam śāpaṇyavratatraḥ. 3). Meaning of the word:\n\nFrom the ocean of words; Anvarah-- not inferior in any way; Mahitve-- in greatness; Tasya-- His (Vyasa's);\nDeha-jah-- from his body; Sarva-atmanah-- with all his mind; Shritah-- taken refuge; Krishnam-- Lord;\nShrikrishna Kaun; Tat-paran-- attached to them; Cha-- and; Api-- also; Anutvraat-tatra- followed..\n\nVidur was the son of Ved Vyas and was no less than him in any way. In this way, they\nWith full devotion, accepted the lotus feet of Shri Krishna, and they were of his devotees"
